1) In java every variable and every expression has some type.
2) Each and every data type is clearly defined.
3) Every assignment should be check by compiler for type compatibility.
4) Because of above reason we can conclude that java language is strongly typed programming language.
5) Java is not considered as pure OOP language because several OOP features are not satisfied by java like operator overloading, multiple inheritance etc.
6) More over we are depending on primitive data types which are non objects.

                                        Primitive data types (8):
Primitive Data Types (P)
────────────────────────
| Category                 | Subcategory              | Data Types             |
|--------------------------|--------------------------|------------------------|
| Numeric Data Types       | Integral Data Types      | byte, short, int, long |
|                          | Floating Point Data Types| float, double          |
|                          |                          |                        |
| Non-numeric Data Types   |                          | char, boolean          |

Except boolean and char remaining data types are considered as signed data types because we can represent both positive and negative numbers

Byte:
1)The size is : 1 byte(8bits)
2)Max_value : +127
3)Min_Value : -128
4)Range : -128 to 127
Byte is best choice if we want to handle data in terms of stream either from the file or from the network(file supported form and network supported form is byte.)

Short:
1)This is the most rarely used data type in java.
2)Size : 2 bytes (16bits)
3)Range : -215 to 215-1 ==> [-32768 to 32767]
Short data type is best suitable for 16 bits processor like 8085 but these processors are completely outdated and hence corresponding short data type is also out dated data type.

Int:
1)The most commonly used data type is int.
2)Size : 4 bytes (32 bits)
3)Range : 231 to 231-1 ==> [-2147483648 to 2147483647]

Long:
1)Sometimes int may not enough to hold big values then we should go for long type.
Ex1: The amount of light travelled in 1000s days. To hold this value int may not be enough then we should go for long data type.
		long l = 126000*60*60*24*1000 miles.
Ex2: The no of present in a big file may exceed int range. Hence the return type of length() is long but not int.
		long l = f.length();
2)Size : 8 bytes(64 bits)
3)Range : -263 to 263-1

Note:
All the above data types(byte, short, int, long) meant for representing integral values. If we want to represent floating point values then we should go for floating point data types.

Floating Point Data Types Comparison:
| Attribute                     | float                              | double                              |
|------------------------------|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|
| Accuracy                     | 5 to 6 decimal places               | 14 to 15 decimal places             |
| Precision Type               | Follows Single precision            | Double precision                    |
| Size                         | 4 bytes                             | 8 bytes                             |
| Range                        | -3.4e38 to 3.4e38                   | -1.7e308 to 1.7e308                 |

Boolean data type:
1)Size : NA(VM dependent)
2)Range : NA but allowed values are true or false.

Char data type:
In old languages like c and c++ are ASCII code based and the no of ASCII code characters are <256 to represent these 256 characters. 8bits are enough hence char size in old languages is 1byte.

In java we are allowed to use any worldwide alphabets character and java is unicode based and no of unicode characters are >256 and <=65536 to represent all these characters 1byte is not enough. Compulsory we should go for 2bytes.

Size: 2 bytes
Range: 0 to 65535
Ex:
char ch1 = 97;
char ch1 = 65536; ---> CE: possible loss of precision.

Note: Null is a default value for object reference and we cant apply for primitives. If we are trying to use for primitive then we will get compile error.